-C. Degree-Budget Completion
-
-B. Wire Detection Benchmark
-
-The endpoint-graph join may leave some component pins
-“floating”: touching only their own component’s net, with no
-wire connecting them to another component. This indicates
-a dropped or over-displaced wire endpoint that the detector
-failed to place near the correct pin.
-Degree-budget completion identifies these floating pins and
-reconnects them via constrained optimization. A pin p is
-treated as floating if its net contains only pins from p’s
-own component, excluding inert types such as gnd, vss, text,
-antenna, junction, and terminal. For each floating pin p, we
-generate candidate edges to all pins q in other components
-within reach r = ρ·s, where ρ is the reach factor; we use ρ = 4
-for the deployed scale-relative join, and Section IV-D shows a
-broad F1 plateau for ρ ∈ [3, 5]. Candidate edge weight is Euclidean distance
-∑︁∥p −q∥. We then solve a min-cost∑︁b-matching
-problem, min (p,q)∈E ′ w(p, q) · xpq subject to q xpq ≤ 1
-for each floating pin p, using the Hungarian algorithm [22]
-(scipy.optimize.linear_sum_assignment). Each
-target pin offers up to three slots so legitimate multi-terminal
-nets are not starved, and a per-pin opt-out leaves a pin with no
-good target unconnected rather than forcing a spurious edge.
-Finally, we reject any matched edge (p, q) if p and q belong
-to the same component or if adding the edge would merge
-two nets that already share a component, which structurally
-prevents short-circuiting two-terminal parts.
-Completion edges are “wireless”: they represent inferred
-connections without corresponding wire segments, analogous
-to manual pin merges in schematic editors. Figure 4 illustrates
-the completion step.
-
-We evaluate on 134 images from CGHD-1152 with groundtruth wire and component labels. Figure 5 and Table I summarize the top configurations across 36 tested variants.
