+\begin{abstract}
+Automated digitization of hand-drawn circuit schematics into SPICE netlists remains challenging because connectivity recovery is brittle under noisy wire detections. We present a deterministic pipeline that converts a scanned schematic into a SPICE netlist through component detection, an occlusion-first wire extractor, and a graph-based join that resolves electrical connectivity. The main contribution is the joining stage: an endpoint-graph model over wire endpoints and component pins with five edge types, combined with degree-budget completion that recovers dropped connections through min-cost $b$-matching while bounding over-merge with per-pin budgets and self-loop guards. We also introduce a synthetic perturbation protocol and, to our knowledge, the first human-verified net-level connectivity benchmark for hand-drawn circuits, covering 31 images from CGHD-1152. The occlusion-first extractor reaches wire-detection F1~=~0.976 on 134 scanned images. On the human-verified net-level benchmark, the best join reaches connectivity micro-F1~=~0.890, outperforming the prior completion default (0.83), a legacy radius union-find join (0.67), a connected-component net-tracing baseline (0.62), and a Hough-plus-proximity line tracer (0.81), all under identical detected wires. Running the join on perfect wire labels does not improve micro-F1, showing that connectivity, not wire detection, remains the bottleneck. A first-party comparison shows a strong vision-language model reaches 0.923 on the same images, statistically indistinguishable from our join but far more expensive and without structural guarantees, supporting explicit geometric connectivity recovery for deployable circuit digitization.
+\end{abstract}
